{"name":"Gracilis","hex":"#c4d5cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gracilis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slender superficial muscle of the inner thigh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gracilis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dorsal column–medial lemniscus pathway","blocks":[{"text":"The dorsal column–medial lemniscus pathway (DCML) (also known as the posterior column-medial lemniscus pathway is the major sensory pathway of the central nervous system that conveys sensations of fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, and proprioception from the skin and joints. It transmits this information to the somatosensory cortex of the postcentral gyrus in the parietal lobe of the brain. The pathway receives information from sensory receptors throughout the body, and carries this in the gracile fasciculus and the cuneate fasciculus, tracts that make up the white matter dorsal columns of the spinal cord. At the level of the medulla oblongata, the fibers of the tracts decussate and are continued in the medial lemniscus, on to the thalamus and relayed from there through the internal capsule and transmitted to the somatosensory cortex. The name dorsal-column medial lemniscus comes from the two structures that carry the sensory information: the dorsal columns of the spinal cord, and the medial lemniscus in the brainstem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Spinal_nerve.svg/330px-Spinal_nerve.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorsal_column%E2%80%93medial_lemniscus_pathway"},{"title":"Gracilis muscle","blocks":[{"text":"The gracilis muscle is the most superficial muscle on the medial side of the thigh. It is thin, flattened and broad above (proximal); narrow and tapered below (distal)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Anterior_Hip_Muscles_2.PNG/330px-Anterior_Hip_Muscles_2.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gracilis_muscle"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Gray759.png/330px-Gray759.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray759.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg/500px-1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg/1280px-1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg/500px-1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neural connections in the DCML pathway.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Gray432_color.png/500px-Gray432_color.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Gray432_color.png/500px-Gray432_color.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray432_color.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Gray235.png/120px-Gray235.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w